\details{
The full conditionals used by \code{\link[=gibbs]{gibbs()}}, exposed so that new samplers (other blockings, cut or
semi-modular schemes, nested chains) can be put together in R while every draw still runs in C++.
All of them use R's random number generator, so \code{set.seed()} makes a composed sampler
reproducible, and a sampler written with them reproduces \code{\link[=gibbs]{gibbs()}} step by step. Notation as in
the samplers: persons j = 1..N, items i = 1..K, \code{Y} the 0/1 responses, \code{L} the log response
times, logit P(y = 1) = a (theta - b), log T = lambda - zeta - rho theta + e.
\itemize{
\item \code{draw_gauss(prec, num)}: independent draws from N(num / prec, 1 / prec), elementwise (any
conditionally normal scalar: ability, speed, rho_i, a shift).
\item \code{draw_invgamma(shape, scale)}, \code{draw_tnorm(mean, sd, lower, upper)}: inverse gamma and
truncated normal draws, elementwise.
\item \code{draw_omega(a, b, theta)}: Pólya–Gamma variables PG(1, a_i (theta_j - b_i)), an N x K matrix.
\item \code{draw_pg(eta)}: Pólya–Gamma variables PG(1, eta) for any matrix of linear predictors (models
whose logit is not a (theta - b), e.g. several abilities or covariates in the item response).
\item \code{draw_probit_z(Y, eta)}: for a probit response model, the latent normal responses of Albert & Chib
(1993), N(eta, 1) truncated to the positive (y = 1) or negative (y = 0) half line; missing y
(NA) are not truncated. Given them, the probit model is a normal linear model.
\item \code{irt_part(Y, omega, a, b)}: the accuracy part of the conditional of
ability given omega, a normal part (precision \code{prec} and linear term \code{num}, see \link{normal_parts}):
\code{draw(irt_part(Y, omega, a, b) + normal_prior(0, 1))}.
\item \code{draw_items_pg(Y, omega, theta, a, priors, one_pl, b_mean)}: b, then a, given omega (Gibbs);
returns \code{list(a, b)}.
\item \code{draw_items_joint(Y, theta, a, b, priors, b_mean)}: (a, b) jointly with omega integrated out
(Metropolis–Hastings with a Fisher-scoring proposal in (a, -a b)); returns \code{list(a, b)} with the
attribute \code{accepted} (one logical per item). It mixes much better than \code{draw_items_pg()} for very
easy or very difficult items; draw omega anew after it.
}

The item priors are those of \code{\link[=rtirt_priors]{rtirt_priors()}} (\code{a}: truncated normal on a > 0, \code{b}: normal);
\code{b_mean} gives b a prior mean per item (e.g. \code{W \%*\% delta} in an LLTM with item covariates \code{W}),
in place of the mean of \code{priors$b}.
\itemize{
\item \code{draw_lambda(L, m, k1nu, W, mu, sd, lower)}: time intensities given the RT means
\code{m} (N x K, e.g. -zeta - rho theta), the ALD shifts \code{k1nu} (zeros for normal times) and the
precisions \code{W} (N x K); prior N(mu, sd^2) on (lower, Inf).
\item \code{draw_sigma2(L, m, lambda, current, priors, nu = NULL, q = NULL)}: residual variances (normal) or
ALD scales (with the mixing variables \code{nu} and the quantile \code{q}), from their \code{current} values
(a half-t prior draws its inverse-gamma auxiliary variable from them).
\item \code{draw_var(alpha, beta, current, prior)}: a variance with likelihood var^-alpha exp(-beta / var)
(n residuals u ~ N(0, var): \code{alpha = n / 2}, \code{beta = sum(u^2) / 2}) and the prior \code{c(df, scale)} (half-t on its square root, two inverse-gamma steps) or \code{c(shape, scale)}
(inverse gamma).
\item \code{draw_nu(r, s, q)}: ALD mixing variables for residuals \code{r} and scales \code{s} (matrices).
\item \code{draw_theta_collapsed(prec, num, W, y, rho, m0, g, tau)} and
\code{draw_rt_items_collapsed(L, k1nu, W, theta, mz, tau, with_rho, mu, sd, rho_sd, lambda, rho)}:
the collapsed steps of \code{gibbs(collapse = TRUE)} (speed integrated out).
}

Missing responses (NA in \code{Y}) are skipped by \code{irt_part()}, \code{draw_items_pg()} and
\code{draw_items_joint()}. \code{draw_lambda()} and \code{draw_sigma2()} need complete log times (they stop on
NA); with missing times use \code{\link[=calc_normal_part]{calc_normal_part()}} with \code{\link[=draw]{draw()}} and \code{draw_var()}. \code{\link[=run_sampler]{run_sampler()}} runs a sampler written from these blocks (chains, burn-in,
storage, summary) and \code{\link[=check_sampler]{check_sampler()}} checks it.

These are low-level: arguments are checked only for their dimensions, and the caller is
responsible for a valid sampler (each block must be drawn from its conditional given the current
values of everything else, or from a marginal followed by the integrated variable).
\code{inst/examples/ex07_blocks.R} writes the \code{rtirt_cross()} sampler with them; \code{ex08_testlet.R},
\code{ex09_mirt_rt.R} and \code{ex10_conditional_dependence.R} compose models from the literature.
}
